<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Kyburg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Counts of Kyburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Kyburg was a comital family of the Swiss plateau, a branch of the counts of Dillingen named after Kyburg Castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a>. In 1218 they inherited much of the Zähringen lands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgdorf">Burgdorf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thun">Thun</a> and Fribourg, and they founded towns such as Winterthur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diessenhofen">Diessenhofen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frauenfeld">Frauenfeld</a>. When Hartmann IV and Hartmann V died without male heirs in 1263–1264, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> took most of their lands.</p></div>
